comments.body:
Apart from when it can't, which is specifically called out as the motivation for this PR:

```
let
  sources = import ./npins;
  nixos = import (sources.nixpkgs + "/nixos")
in
nixos {
  configuration.imports  = [
     { _module.args = { inherit sources; }; }
     { sources, ... }: {
       import = [ sources.modules ];
     }
  ];
}
```

This doesn't work. It can't work. It does work with specialArgs. If specialArgs is niche, why is it exposed in `lib.nixosSystem`? Seems to me it exists to do exactly what this is _trying_ to do. I mean the answer is in the comment you are quoting:

> This should only be used for special arguments that need to be evaluated when resolving module structure (like in imports)

EDIT: fwiw I am happy to be told how I can achieve the above in a different way. I don't want to die on the hill of this patch, I just want a way to have nice configuration with out experimental features.

> If specialArgs is niche, why is it exposed in `lib.nixosSystem`?

Dunno, I wasn't using nixos [in 2015 when it was introduced](https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/commit/b13cb5461433ccf7ae1a2e8d2691c3111441e984). Doesn't mean I think it's a great design pattern to have two things that do almost the same thing with slightly different nuances, nor do I want to encourage it.

Your example isn't valid syntax but whatever, I'll assume you meant

```nix
let
  sources = import ./npins;
  nixos = import (sources.nixpkgs + "/nixos");
in
nixos {
  configuration.imports = [
    { _module.args = { inherit sources; }; }
    (
      { sources, ... }:
      {
        imports = [ (import sources.modules) ];
      }
    )
  ];
}
```

which infrecs. But `sources` is already in scope due to the earlier binding, so you could write

```nix
let
  sources = import ./npins;
  nixos = import (sources.nixpkgs + "/nixos");
in
nixos {
  configuration.imports = [
    { _module.args = { inherit sources; }; }
    {
      imports = [ (import sources.modules) ];
    }
  ];
}
```

EDIT: I'm also just... not convinced of the intent to put modules into module args. Which is why I wouldn't.
